course: everyday
lesson: 94
title: Frustration, loneliness, guilt and living with siblings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Frustration is the hot, stuck feeling when something will not work; a short break, a fresh look and asking for help usually beat giving up, because a rested brain sees what it missed.
explain: Loneliness is feeling that nobody is with you; other children often feel it too, and clubs, teams, buddy benches and keeping in touch with people far away all help.
explain: Guilt is the bad feeling after you have hurt someone; it shows that you care, and saying sorry and doing something kind lets it go.
explain: Brothers and sisters share a home, so fair rules agreed together (taking turns, knocking, quiet times) stop the same fights happening again and again.
explain: A boy in Ohio cannot finish his Lego spaceship; he has a drink, looks at the instructions again, spots a missing piece on page 12, and the ship holds together at last.
fact: When you are stuck, a short break and reading the question again help, and then you can ask for help.
fact: Most builders have to try many times before a model works.
fact: Other children often feel lonely at break time too, and a buddy bench or club can help.
fact: Clubs, teams and library groups help you make friends in a new town.
fact: Letters, video calls and a calendar help when a parent works far away or is deployed (sent away to work) in the military.
fact: Guilt shows that you care, and saying sorry and being kind helps fix things.
fact: Younger siblings copy older ones because they admire them and learn by watching.
fact: Fair rules, like a timer for turns, stop the same argument happening again.
fact: Everyone deserves some private space, so ask siblings calmly to knock.
fact: Tell an adult about a sibling only when someone is hurt, in danger or breaking an important rule.
fact: Parents love each child, but they may not know how you feel unless you tell them.
fact: Older siblings need time with friends their own age.
fact: A secret that makes you worry about someone's safety is told to a trusted adult, even if you promised to keep it.
fact: When parents say they «need some time apart», they live in different homes for a while to sort things out; it is never the child's fault.
fact: Boys and girls can be just friends; teasing that a friend is your boyfriend or girlfriend is not funny if it upsets you, and a parent can help.
exercise: Ella is stuck on hard homework and wants to give up. What should she do first? | Take a short break, read it again, then ask for help | Rip up the page and say she cannot do it | Copy all the answers from the back of the book and hand it in | Keep staring at it until bedtime
exercise: Why does a short break help with a hard problem? | A rested brain notices things it missed | The problem gets easier by itself | It makes the teacher give an easier one | It lets you forget the homework
exercise: Tom's Lego model keeps falling apart. What is the best plan? | Check the instructions, try another way and ask for help if needed | Throw the pieces back in the box for good | Glue all the pieces together with strong glue so they never move again | Blame his sister for breaking it on purpose
exercise: Aisha feels lonely at break time. What could she do? | Look for someone else alone or sit on the buddy bench | Stay inside the classroom every break | Wait for someone to come and find her | Tell herself that nobody will ever like her, so there is no point trying
exercise: Leo's family has moved to a new town and he has no friends there. What helps most? | Join a club or team and say hello to children nearby | Stay indoors on his own until he gets used to living in the new town | Only talk to his old friends online | Wait for children to knock on his door
exercise: Maya's dad is deployed with the army for months. How can she stay close? | Write letters, video call and mark the days on a calendar | Try not to think about him at all | Stop talking about him so nobody is sad | Wait until he comes back home before telling him anything about her life
exercise: Ben was mean to a friend and now feels bad. What should he do? | Say sorry and do something kind to make up | Avoid the friend for a while so that he forgets all about it | Wait for the friend to say sorry first | Pretend it was only a joke
exercise: What does a feeling of guilt show? | That you care about how others feel | That you are a bad person | That you must be punished | That the other person was wrong and deserved it
exercise: Sophie's little brother copies everything she does. Why does he do it? | He admires her and learns by watching her | He is trying to make her angry on purpose | He has no ideas of his own at all | He wants to get her into trouble
exercise: Two sisters argue every day about whose turn it is on the tablet. What helps? | Use a timer or agreed turns, or ask a parent to decide | The older one should always go first | Whoever shouts loudest gets the next turn | Hide the tablet somewhere so that nobody at all is able to use it again
exercise: Your sibling keeps coming into your room without asking. What is best? | Ask calmly for them to knock and agree house rules | Lock them out of the bathroom next time to get even with them | Go into their room and mess it up | Shout at them every time they come in
exercise: Josh's sister is told off by their dad and Josh wants to laugh. What should he do? | Not laugh or tease her | Laugh, since she deserved it | Tell his friends about it | Remind her about it later
exercise: When should you tell an adult about something your sibling did? | When someone is hurt, in danger or breaking an important rule | Every time they do anything at all that you do not like or agree with | Only when you want them to get in trouble | Never, because siblings must keep secrets
exercise: Two brothers share a bedroom and keep arguing. What helps? | Agree on quiet times, light-off times and private corners | Let the older one make all the rules for the bedroom on his own | Split the room with a line of toys | Each try to keep the other awake
exercise: Lucy feels her parents love her sister more. What should she do? | Tell her parents how she feels | Keep the feeling secret and sulk | Be mean to her sister to get noticed | Decide it must be true and give up
exercise: Sam's brother uses a wheelchair and gets a lot of help. Sam feels left out. What helps? | Ask questions, help where he can and tell his parents how he feels | Keep quiet because his brother's needs come first | Pretend he does not mind at all | Stay away from his brother as much as possible so that he is not jealous
exercise: Your older sister will not let you join her and her friends. What should you do? | Find your own fun and do something with her another time | Follow them around until they let you in | Tell your parents to make her include you every time her friends come | Break her things so she has to notice you
test: A girl in Calgary has tried a hard puzzle for half an hour and wants to scream. What is wisest? | Stop for a few minutes, then look again or ask for help | Keep forcing the pieces that do not fit | Throw the puzzle and never do one again | Say puzzles are stupid and walk away from it for good, never trying again
test: Which is true about loneliness? | Many children feel it, and clubs and buddies can help | Only children with no family feel lonely | Lonely children should wait to be found | Loneliness means that nobody will ever like you, however hard you try
test: Why does saying sorry help when you feel guilty? | It starts to fix things, so the guilt can go | It makes the other person forget what happened | It means you will never be told off | It shows the other person was wrong too
test: A boy in Melbourne gets cross that his little sister copies his dance. What is a kinder way to see it? | She copies him because she looks up to him | She copies him on purpose just to make him look silly | She has no ideas of her own at all | She does it only to annoy their parents
test: Two cousins fight over the swing every visit. Which idea will most likely stop it for good? | Agree a fair rule, such as five minutes each with a timer | The one who gets there first keeps the swing for the whole day | Let them fight until one gives up | Take the swing down so nobody uses it
test: Your brother took one of your pencils without asking. Is this something to tell an adult straight away? | No, it is small; ask him calmly to give it back | Yes, report every small thing he does | Yes, so that he gets punished | No, but take two of his pencils instead to make it fair
test: A girl's mum is working on an oil rig for a month. Which helps most? | Video calls and a calendar counting down the days | Not mentioning her mum so nobody gets sad | Staying angry with everyone at home until her mum comes home again | Pretending her mum is just at the shops
test: Why should you knock before going into a sibling's room? | Everyone deserves some private space | It is only polite if a guest is there | Knocking keeps the door from breaking | Only so they can hide what they are doing
test: Mia thinks her parents love her brother more because he gets more help with reading. What is most likely true? | They love both, and he simply needs more help right now | They do love him more than they love her | She must have done something bad to make them stop loving her | Parents always love the youngest child best
test: this sum's impossible, been on it ages, i give up | Take a short break, read it again, then ask for help | Give up, some sums just cannot be done by anybody | Rip up the page and start over | Copy the answer from a friend
test: nobody plays w me at break since we moved | Try the buddy bench or join a club | Stay alone, nobody ever wants to play with new kids | Hide in the toilets at break | Wait till they come to you
test: lil bro copies everything i do, sooo annoying | He looks up to you and learns by watching | He is only doing it on purpose to make you angry | He has no ideas of his own | He wants to be better than you
test: sis came in my room w/o knocking again | Ask her calmly to knock | Lock her out of the house | Go in her room and mess it up | Scream until mum comes running
explain: A boy in Leeds got so frustrated with his maths homework that he tore up his book; he had to redo all the pages and still did not understand the sums until he asked for help.
explain: A girl in Canada felt lonely at her new school and started talking online to a stranger who said he was a kid; he was an adult who asked for photos, and she told her mum, who stopped it and reported him.
fact: Lonely children are sometimes targeted online by adults pretending to be friends.
fact: When something is frustrating, a short break and asking for help work better than giving up or breaking things.
test: new online mate says he's 11 and wants pics, i'm kinda lonely | Don't send any, tell a trusted adult straight away | Send one, he's really nice to you and you're lonely | Send one but don't show your face | Ask him for his pics first
test: homework so hard, gonna rip it up | Take a break, then ask for help | Rip it up and just tell the teacher u lost it | Copy a mate's answers | Never try maths again
test: little sis keeps taking my stuff, gonna hit her | Don't hit her, tell a parent and keep your stuff safe | Hit her so she learns to leave my stuff alone | Take all her toys away and hide them from her | Lock her in her room
test: my friend says her online friend "Jess, 11" wants to meet up alone n not tell parents, she's met loads of girls like that | Tell a trusted adult; someone online asking for a secret meeting may be an adult pretending | Go, but only if Jess sends a nice photo first and promises she is really 11 years old | Go, it is fine if she has met other girls | Keep it secret, as Jess asked
test: heard Ollie smashed his Lego model to bits cos it fell apart, like 2000 pieces, n his mum had to hoover it all up | Frustration is better handled with a break and asking for help; 2000 pieces is probably exaggerated | Smashing it was the right thing to do, because a broken model is useless anyway | Builders never have to try more than once | Frustration always ends in smashing things
test: the class says Sophie's always on her own at break cos nobody likes her, should i tell the new kid that | No, do not spread it; many children feel lonely, and you could invite her to join in | Yes, so that the new kid knows straight away which child to avoid at break time | Yes, but only if the new kid asks about her | Yes, because it must be true if she is alone
exam: Which of the following measures is recommended when a pupil becomes stuck upon a task? | A short break, a fresh reading of the question, and then a request for help | Abandoning the task permanently and declaring that it cannot be done | Breaking the materials so that the task need not be attempted again | Copying the answer of another pupil without making any attempt to understand it
exam: Identify the circumstances in which a child ought to inform an adult about the conduct of a sibling. | When someone is hurt, in danger, or breaking an important rule | Whenever the sibling does anything at all which happens to be annoying | Never, since all matters between siblings must remain secret | Only when the sibling has refused to share a toy or a game
exam: Pursuant to the observation that a younger sibling copies an older one, select the explanation consistent with the established facts. | Younger siblings admire older ones and learn by watching them | Younger siblings copy older ones solely in order to annoy them | Younger siblings copy older ones because they are told to do so | Younger siblings copy older ones only when parents are absent
exam: In respect of a family in which a parent works far away or is deployed in the military, which measures assist the child? | Letters, video calls and a calendar | Avoiding any mention of the parent | Refusing to speak during video calls | Waiting in silence for the return
test: my sis keeps sneaking out "to see someone" n made me promise not to tell, feels heavy | She may be seeing a boyfriend or girlfriend; if the secret worries you about her safety, tell a trusted adult | Keep the secret forever no matter what happens, because a promise to a sister can never be broken | Tell everyone at school about it, so that lots of people can keep an eye on her | Follow her out at night
test: lonely since Dad moved out, mum says they "need some time apart" | Your parents are living apart for a while to sort things out; it is not your fault, so tell a trusted adult how you feel | Dad moved out because of something you did, so you need to be extra good to make him come home | It means Dad has just gone on a long holiday on his own, and he will be back very soon | Keep it secret and be alone
test: my bro teases me "u got a boyfriend" when i just played with Max | He is teasing that Max is your boyfriend; Max is just a friend, so tell him it is not funny and ask a parent to help | You must stop playing with Max straight away, because boys and girls cannot just be friends | It is true because he says so | Hit your brother hard so that he learns to stop teasing you
